WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.629
Speaker 1: Hey, David, do you think Mitch
McConnell and Republicans will be punished

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:09.680
for openly obstructing everything Joe Biden
does, as Mitch McConnell has promised to do?

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.290
I wish I could tell you yes, the truth is
Mitch McConnell might be rewarded for obstructing

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.900
everything that Joe Biden tries to do.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.820
There have been no political consequences
for Mitch McConnell and the Republicans of

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:29.579
2016 for blocking the Merrick Garland Supreme
Court nomination made by Barack Obama.

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:33.940
And not only that, many Republicans absolutely
freakin love it.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:40.300
So we I mean, listen, the framing of the question
is correct in that Mitch McConnell has said

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.460
he's not going to compromise.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.170
Mitch McConnell has said he's not Republicans
aren't going to vote for anything that Joe

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.870
Biden wants to do.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.540
We saw it with covid stimulus.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.210
Democrats had to go it completely alone.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.480
It looks like it's going to be that way with
infrastructure.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.579
These Republicans want nothing to do with
the covid, the jobs bill and on and on and

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:02.579
on.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:05.370
Now, there's a couple of different ways that
it could go.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.560
One is these things are popular.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.460
Covid stimulus was popular.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.290
Infrastructure is popular.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:15.290
If Republican voters see that, hey, we got
this stuff and we like this stuff, but not

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.600
a single Republican voted for it.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.580
They may say, let's punish these Republicans
in the fall.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.330
Because if it were up to them, we would have
gotten nothing at the same time, others would

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:28.570
say, hey, you know what?

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:33.870
What, I still got the stuff and I don't even
have to vote for Democrats, so I'll just keep

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.380
voting for Republicans.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:40.270
That's actually a rationalization we may see
among many of these Republican voters.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:46.370
I think the biggest risk to Mitch McConnell
and Republicans in their obstructionism is

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:52.020
that a couple of Republican senators could
be Mitt Romney, could be, you know, the same

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.950
people that perennially disappoint us.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:02.990
Lisa Murkowski, Susan Collins, if a couple
of them say these are simple needed things

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:08.640
that Joe Biden wants to do, I'm going to join
the Democrats, assuming Democrats have all

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.480
50 of theirs, including Joe Manchin and Kirsten
Sinema, and get a couple of Republicans that

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:19.950
could start to hurt Mitch McConnell, where
you start seeing Republicans abandoning Mitch

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.690
and saying now a lot of this stuff just makes
sense to do.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.080
We need to do the infrastructure.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.200
I like the jobs bill, etc.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.360
That's the biggest risk.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:38.330
But Republicans, if they remain unified, can
withhold any appearance of bipartisanship

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.220
in order to try to harm Joe Biden and Democrats.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.030
And it could actually work.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:49.209
There's this you know, bipartisanship in a
vacuum pulls well with voters.

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:54.730
And so while my view of bipartisanship is
the president should do things that the American

00:02:54.730 --> 00:03:00.730
people want, if 70 percent of Americans want
covid relief, it doesn't really matter.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.030
If you don't have enough Senate votes, you
should do covid relief.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.910
That definition of bipartisanship is very
favorable to Joe Biden.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:15.080
The counter is that there are some Americans
and again, I think that people just working

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.900
jobs and feeding their families, they don't
care about this stuff.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:24.960
But there are people who do believe that bipartisanship
means you've got to have some Republicans

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.700
voting for what you're doing.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.099
And if Republicans can remain unified, there
might be enough of those people in the Republican

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:35.830
Party who would say Joe Biden did it, but
he didn't do it the right way with covid cash.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:36.830
It's not a factor.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.110
I've not heard anyone say I got my fourteen
hundred bucks, but I don't want it because,

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.990
you know, Mitch McConnell didn't vote for
it.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:46.310
Come on, guys, give me a break.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:48.360
But with some of these other bills, that could
become a factor.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:54.090
So we're going to see the question of whether
Mitch McConnell will be punished for the open.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.010
Obstruction is a good one.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:59.670
The question of whether Mitch McConnell will
be rewarded for the open obstruction is just

00:03:59.670 --> 00:03:59.689
as good of a question.

